    body: `RYA recognition is the badge that tells learners, parents, schools and insurers that your centre teaches to a national standard. It opens the door to issuing RYA certificates, appearing in the RYA's "Where's my nearest" search, and being taken seriously by partners such as local authorities and the Scouts. It also commits you to a way of working: named roles, written procedures, inspected premises and a culture of keeping records. This article walks through the whole journey, from the first conversation to your annual renewal, and flags the places where new centres most often stumble.

## Decide which schemes you want to teach

Recognition is granted per scheme, not as a single blanket licence. A dinghy centre, a powerboat school, a windsurfing school and a shorebased navigation provider are each recognised separately, each with their own staffing, equipment and site requirements. Start by being honest about what you can deliver well in your first season. Most new centres begin with one or two schemes, usually the National Sailing Scheme and the Youth Sailing Scheme for dinghies, or Powerboat Level 1 and 2. Adding windsurfing, paddlesport, keelboat or yacht cruising later is straightforward once your systems are proven.

Each scheme has an instructor qualification that your lead person must hold, a set of boats and safety craft you must be able to put on the water, and a sailing area that suits the courses. Read the recognition guidance for each scheme you are considering and list what you already have against what it asks for. The gaps are your pre-application to-do list.

## Appoint the people the RYA needs to see

Every centre needs a Principal, who carries overall responsibility for the centre and its compliance, and a Chief Instructor for each scheme, who holds the relevant instructor qualification and oversees teaching standards. In a small centre one person often wears both hats. That is fine, but it only works if that person is genuinely present and in control during operations.

Decide early who these people are and make sure their own qualifications, first aid, safeguarding training and any vetting are current before you apply. The inspector will ask to see them. If your Chief Instructor is a seasonal hire, you need a plan for who holds the role in winter when you are still offering shorebased or powerboat courses.

## Get the site, boats and safety cover right

Recognition looks closely at the physical side: a safe launch and recovery area, a sailing area appropriate to the courses you will run, changing and briefing space, toilets, and the ability to call help. Your boats must be suitable for teaching, maintained and in sufficient number for your planned group sizes, and you need powered safety cover that matches those groups and the water you sail on.

Write down your planned maximum group sizes and the safety boat and instructor cover that goes with them. These ratios become part of your operating procedures and the inspector will test whether you can actually meet them. Over-promising here is the most common cause of a conditional report.

## Write the paperwork so it describes what you actually do

You will need operating procedures, risk assessments, an emergency action plan, a safeguarding policy and a child protection policy where relevant, equal opportunities and complaints procedures, and evidence of insurance. The temptation is to download templates and change the name at the top. Inspectors can tell, and more importantly your staff will not use documents that do not match your real site.

Walk your site with a notebook and describe each part of the day in order: arrival and sign-in, briefing, kitting up, launching, on-the-water supervision, recovery, debrief and departure. Then write procedures for each of those stages. Add the specific hazards of your water, such as a tidal gate, a ferry route, a weir or a busy slipway. Keep the language plain. A good test is whether a newly arrived instructor could read the procedures on Monday morning and run Tuesday safely.

## Make the application

Applications go through the RYA and your regional development officer or the training department will be in touch to arrange an inspection. You will be asked for your scheme choices, named roles, site details, boat and equipment lists, sample documents and proof of insurance. Expect questions. Respond quickly and completely, because the inspection is usually booked only once the paperwork is in order.

Before the inspection date, run your centre as if it were already recognised. Operate a real session using your procedures and your sign-in process, with your logbooks and incident forms in use. The inspector wants to see a working centre, not a dress rehearsal.

## The inspection day

An inspection typically covers a tour of the site, a review of boats, safety craft and equipment, a check of the documents and records, conversations with the Principal and Chief Instructor, and often a look at a session in progress. Inspectors are experienced instructors who want you to succeed. Treat it as a free consultancy visit. Ask questions about anything you are unsure of and write down the advice you receive.

You will receive a report with any conditions or recommendations. Conditions must be met, usually within a set time, before recognition is confirmed. Recommendations are strongly worth acting on because they tend to reappear as conditions next year if ignored.

## Keeping recognition live

Recognition is renewed annually, and you will be inspected on a regular cycle. In between, you are expected to keep your named roles filled, your instructor qualifications and first aid current, your boats maintained, your incident records up to date, and your procedures reviewed at least once a year. Changes to your Principal, Chief Instructor, site or schemes must be notified.

The centres that find renewal easy are the ones whose records are kept as part of the normal week rather than assembled in a panic. A simple register of staff certificates with expiry dates, a dated maintenance log for each boat, a signed-off risk assessment review each spring, and a tidy file of incident reports with the follow-up actions recorded will answer most of an inspector's questions before they are asked.

## Common pitfalls for new centres

- Applying before the lead instructor's own qualifications and first aid are in date.
- Planning group sizes that the available safety cover cannot support.
- Procedures copied from another centre that reference boats or waters you do not have.
- No clear plan for who is in charge when the Principal is away.
- Forgetting that shorebased courses, powerboat and dinghy may each need their own recognition.
- Treating the inspection as the finish line rather than the start of an annual cycle.

## A realistic timeline

From first enquiry to confirmed recognition, allow three to six months. Much of that time is spent on your side: gathering documents, finishing certificates and sorting out boats. Centres that start the process in the autumn are usually recognised in time for the spring season. Starting in April and hoping to open in May rarely works.

Recognition is work, but it is also the foundation for everything else: the courses you can sell, the instructors you can attract and the trust your community places in you. Do it properly once and the renewals look after themselves.`,
