    body: `Every RYA centre has operating procedures. Far fewer have operating procedures that their instructors have read, remember and follow on a busy Saturday. The difference is not the quality of the writing so much as the purpose behind it. Procedures written to satisfy an inspection become a document that sits in a folder. Procedures written to help a new instructor run a safe session become the backbone of how the centre works. This article is about writing the second kind.

## Start from the day, not from a template

Templates are useful for checking you have covered everything, but they are a poor starting point. Begin instead with a walk through a normal operating day at your centre, from the first person arriving to the last boat put away. At each stage ask three questions: what has to happen, who makes it happen, and what could go wrong here that we need to guard against. Write the answers in order.

This produces a document shaped like your centre rather than like a generic one. It naturally captures the things that make your site different: the slipway that floods on springs, the ferry that crosses your sailing area at ten past the hour, the strong offshore wind that funnels down the valley in the afternoon. Those are precisely the things a template cannot know and an inspector most wants to see addressed.

## Separate the levels of detail

A single document that tries to do everything ends up too long to read. Split your procedures into layers:

- **Standing procedures**: the things that are always true. Roles and responsibilities, sign-in and sign-out, buoyancy aid policy, communications, group sizes and safety cover ratios, when the Chief Instructor must be consulted.
- **Activity procedures**: a short section per activity, such as dinghy sailing, powerboating, windsurfing or tasters, covering briefing points, equipment checks, launching, the sailing area and limits for each group level.
- **Emergency action plan**: a separate, short, laminated document covering capsize and entrapment, person overboard from a safety boat, missing person, medical emergency, fire, and severe weather, with the phone numbers and grid references the coastguard or ambulance will ask for.
- **Site-specific hazards**: a one or two page list, ideally with a map, of the local hazards and the rules that address each one.

Instructors can then carry the parts they need. The emergency plan lives in every safety boat and on the office wall. The activity procedure for the day's course gets reread during setup.

## Write for the newest instructor on the worst day

Picture a competent but unfamiliar instructor who has joined you this week, teaching on a day when the Principal is off site and the weather is marginal. Every sentence should help that person make the right call. That means being concrete about limits. "Sail in suitable conditions" helps no one. "Beginner groups do not launch above 15 knots sustained or if the Chief Instructor has set a red flag; improvers may sail to 20 knots within the inner area with a safety boat in attendance" tells the instructor exactly where the line is.

It also means saying who decides. If the launch decision rests with the Senior Instructor on duty, say so and say how they record it. Ambiguity about authority is a common root cause in incident investigations.

## Keep each procedure short

A procedure that runs to several pages will not be followed in full. Aim for each activity procedure to fit on one or two sides, written as short numbered steps or checklists. Put explanations and background in an appendix if you must, but keep the operational steps clean. Checklists in particular are powerful for the moments where people are rushed: kit checks before launching, safety boat checks each morning, the end-of-day count of people, boats and keys.

## Make the ratios and limits explicit

The numbers in your procedures are the ones that will be tested, by an inspector and, in the worst case, by a court. For each activity and level, state the maximum number of learners per instructor, the number and type of safety craft required, the number of qualified people who must be on the water, the sailing area and the weather limits. Where RYA guidance gives a range, write down the figure you have chosen and why. Where you operate more conservatively than the guidance, say so; that is a strength.

Review these figures each year against your actual fleet and staff. A ratio that assumed two safety boats is unsafe the season one of them is off the water for repairs, and your procedures need to say what happens then.

## Involve the people who will use them

Write a draft, then give it to two or three of your instructors with a red pen. Ask them where it does not match what actually happens and where it is unclear. Instructors are often following unwritten local practice that is better than the document, and you want that captured. They are also often doing things the document does not allow, which you need to know.

Once the procedures are agreed, make reading them part of induction, and have each instructor sign to confirm they have read the current version. Keep that record with their staff file.

## Train, test and tweak

A procedure that has only ever been read is a weak one. Run a short drill each pre-season for the key emergency scenarios: a capsize with a trapped sailor, a missing person after a tide change, a medical incident on the far side of the lake. Time the response and note where people hesitated. Those hesitations are usually a sign that the written procedure is unclear or that a piece of equipment is in the wrong place.

After any incident or near miss, go back to the relevant procedure and ask whether it helped. Update it if not, note the change in a version log, and tell the team.

## Version control without bureaucracy

Put a version number and date on every document and keep one master copy, ideally somewhere everyone can read online as well as the printed copies in the boats and office. When you change something, update the master, reprint the affected pages and remove the old ones. Keep superseded versions in an archive folder; if you ever need to show what the procedure said on a particular date, you will be grateful.

## A simple structure to copy

1. Purpose and scope, half a page.
2. Roles, responsibilities and who decides what.
3. Daily routine: opening, sign-in, briefings, launching, supervision, recovery, closing.
4. Group sizes, safety cover and weather limits by activity and level.
5. Equipment standards and checks.
6. Site hazards and local rules, with a map.
7. Communications: radios, channels, phones, signals.
8. Emergency action plan, as a stand-alone card.
9. Incident and near-miss reporting.
10. Review history.

Procedures written this way are shorter, more honest and more useful than the thick folder most centres inherit. They also make inspections easy, because the inspector can see a document that describes the centre in front of them. Most of all, they give your team confidence on the days when confidence matters.`,
