    tags: ["equipment", "fleet", "budgeting"],
    coverEmoji: "⛵",
    imageQuery: "sailing dinghies lined up on beach fleet",
    body: `The boats you buy in your first few seasons decide what you can teach, how many people you can take out, how much of your week goes on maintenance and how much cash is tied up on the slipway. Get it right and your fleet quietly earns its keep for ten years. Get it wrong and you spend every spring repairing boats nobody wanted to sail. This guide is for Principals and committees making fleet decisions, whether starting from nothing or renewing a tired inventory.

## Begin with the courses, not the boats

Write down the courses you intend to run over the next three years and the typical learner for each one. A centre teaching mostly adult beginners and improvers has very different needs from one running a junior pathway from Stage 1 to a racing squad. Then ask, for each course, what boat teaches it best, how many learners it carries, and how many you need on the water at once to run a full group with the ratios in your procedures.

This produces a fleet plan rather than a wish list. It also shows where one boat type can cover several courses, which is where your money goes furthest.

## The core dinghy fleet

For most centres, the backbone is a stable, forgiving double-hander for beginners and a lighter, more responsive boat for improvers and juniors. Rotomoulded or roto-plastic boats take knocks from novice crews and need little cosmetic care, which matters when the same hull does five sessions a week. Fibreglass boats sail better and hold their value, but every gelcoat chip is a job. Many centres run a plastic beginner fleet and a smaller number of fibreglass boats for improver and racing courses.

Think about rig flexibility. Boats that can be sailed with a reduced rig let you keep teaching nervous beginners in a breeze that would otherwise cancel the session. Boats that can be single-handed or double-handed stretch across more of your programme.

## Safety craft

Your safety boats are the one part of the fleet where under-buying is dangerous. You need enough powered craft to meet your safety cover ratios on your busiest planned day with one boat unavailable, because one always is. RIBs are the common choice: stable, quick, easy to bring alongside a capsized dinghy and forgiving of a hard landing on a beach. Engine size should suit your water; a lake centre does not need the horsepower a tidal estuary centre does, but every safety boat must be able to tow a swamped dinghy and its crew back against the worst wind and tide you operate in.

Buy the kill cords, radios, throw lines, first aid kits, paddles and anchors at the same time as the boat and fit them properly. A safety boat that is missing its kit is not safety cover.

## Powerboat training craft

If you intend to run powerboat courses, decide whether your safety boats double as training boats. Many centres do this successfully, but it means your safety cover is reduced whenever a powerboat course is running, and your operating procedures must reflect that. Boats used for training wear faster than boats used for cover, so budget for it.

## Windsurfing, paddlesport and keelboats

Each extra discipline brings its own fleet. Windsurfing needs a range of board volumes and sail sizes to match learner weights and wind strengths; buying only one size leaves half your group ashore. Stand-up paddleboards are cheap and popular but need storage and transport thought. Keelboats bring a step change in cost, mooring and maintenance and are rarely sensible until the dinghy operation is well established.

## Buying new versus second-hand

New boats come with warranties, consistent fleets and the ability to spread cost through finance or a sponsored fleet deal from a builder. Second-hand boats can halve your capital outlay and the market in ex-centre boats is active, but inspect carefully. Look for soft decks, worn centreboard cases, cracked gunwales, corroded fittings and sails that have lost their shape. Ask to see the maintenance history. A boat that has been in a well-run centre for five years may be in better order than a privately owned boat that has sat on a trolley.

Consistency has real value. Six identical boats mean one set of spares, one rigging guide, interchangeable parts and no arguments about who gets the good one. Where possible, buy in matched groups.

## Count the whole cost

The sticker price is perhaps half the true cost of a boat over its life. Add trolleys and road trailers, covers, spare sails, foils and rudders, the annual service of outboards, antifoul or storage, insurance, and the instructor and volunteer hours that go into keeping it ready. Plastic boats cost less to maintain but depreciate to very little; fibreglass boats cost more to keep but sell on. Model both over ten years before you decide.

## Storage and handling

A fleet you cannot store or move safely is a liability. Measure your dinghy park, racks and shed before you order. Consider how many people it takes to launch each boat and whether juniors can handle it. Heavy boats that need three adults to get down a slipway slow every session and tire your staff. Trolleys with the right wheels for your beach or slip are worth every penny.

## Plan the renewal cycle

Write a simple fleet register: each boat, its year, its condition, its intended retirement date and what will replace it. Rotate the oldest boats out of the beginner fleet into less demanding roles, then sell them while they still have value. Spreading renewal over several years keeps the capital bill manageable and avoids the shock of a whole fleet reaching the end of its life at once.

## Equipment beyond the boats

Buoyancy aids in a full range of sizes, including small child and large adult, replaced on a schedule and inspected each season. Wetsuits if you provide them, with a drying and hygiene system that actually works. Radios with enough handsets for every instructor and boat, plus chargers and spares. Whiteboards, model boats and shelter for briefings. Launching trolleys, fuel storage that meets regulations, tool kits and a stocked spares cupboard. None of these are glamorous and all of them stop sessions when they are missing.

## Questions to ask before you sign

- Which courses will this boat teach, and for how many years?
- How many do we need to run a full group under our ratios?
- Can our staff and learners launch and recover it safely on our site?
- What will it cost per year to keep ready, and what will it be worth when we sell it?
- Do we have the storage, trolleys and spares to go with it?
- Does buying this delay something more important, such as a second safety boat?

A fleet chosen this way tends to be smaller, more consistent and better maintained than one assembled opportunistically. It also tends to be the fleet that lets you say yes to the courses your customers actually ask for.`,
