    body: `Sailing centres in the UK and Ireland live by the calendar. Most of the year's income arrives between Easter and the end of September, the weather dictates what can be delivered week to week, and instructors come and go with the academic year. Centres that plan their year as a whole, rather than lurching from one busy weekend to the next, run calmer seasons, make more money and keep their staff. Here is a way to map the year that works for commercial schools and clubs alike.

## Work backwards from the peak

Start by identifying your genuine peak: for most centres the school summer holidays, plus the May and August bank holiday weeks and any local regatta or festival. Everything else in the plan exists to make that peak run well. Count the number of teaching days in it, the maximum number of groups you can run each day with your fleet and site, and the instructors and safety boats each day requires. That gives you the staffing and equipment target the rest of the year must deliver.

## Autumn: review and decide

The best time to plan next season is the month after this one ends, while the lessons are fresh. Pull together course fill rates, cancellations, incident reports, customer feedback and instructor exit conversations. Look for the patterns: which courses sold out and which limped, which weeks were short of staff, which boats were off the water most, which complaints repeated.

From that, decide the course programme for next year. Fix dates for the big items first: summer camps, bank holiday courses, instructor training weeks, regattas. Publish them early, because families book summer holidays in the autumn and schools plan residentials a year ahead. Decide what you will stop running as well as what you will add.

## Winter: build the machine

Winter is when the operation is made ready. The list is long and best handled as a project with owners and dates:

- **Fleet**: strip, inspect and repair every boat, service every outboard, replace worn sails and foils, update the fleet register with what was done.
- **Site**: slipway, pontoons, racks, fuel store, changing rooms, signage.
- **Paperwork**: review operating procedures and risk assessments, update the emergency plan, renew insurance, submit RYA recognition renewal, review your safeguarding and data protection policies.
- **People**: recruit instructors, confirm returners, run first aid and safeguarding refreshers, book instructor CPD and any revalidation.
- **Commercial**: set prices, open bookings, plan the marketing calendar, confirm school and group partnerships.
- **Revenue**: shorebased theory courses, powerboat courses in sheltered water, equipment servicing for members, and winter maintenance programmes for volunteers all help smooth cash flow.

## Early spring: pre-season

Four to six weeks before opening, move from planning to readiness. Get boats rigged and on the water for a shakedown, run safety boat drills, hold the staff induction, walk the site with the emergency plan, test radios and phones, and run at least one full dress rehearsal session with staff as learners. Finish the roster for the first month so instructors know when they are needed. Check that every certificate the season depends on is in date and that any vetting checks have come back.

## The season: run it, do not re-plan it

Once the season starts, the Principal's job is to keep the plan on track rather than redesign it. A weekly rhythm helps: a short staff meeting at the start of the week to walk through the roster, the weather outlook and any issues; a mid-week check of bookings and staffing for the next fortnight; a Friday review of incidents, feedback and maintenance jobs that arose. Keep a running list of things to change next year rather than trying to fix structural problems in July.

Build slack in deliberately. Instructors need days off, boats need a day for repair, and the weather will cost you sessions. A plan that assumes every day runs full with everyone fit is a plan that fails in week three.

## Staffing across the year

The core challenge of seasonal planning is matching a workforce that is mostly students and seasonal workers to a demand curve that peaks when they are available and collapses when they go back to university. Map your staffing needs week by week across the year. Then decide which roles need year-round people, usually the Principal, Chief Instructor and perhaps a maintenance and office role, and which can be seasonal. Offer your best seasonal instructors first refusal on next year's dates before Christmas; the good ones are booked by Easter.

## Watch the cash, not just the calendar

Seasonality is a cash-flow problem as much as an operating one. Most of your costs for the season, including maintenance, insurance, deposits for new boats and early staff wages, land before the income does. Build a simple month-by-month cash forecast, take deposits on bookings, use early-bird pricing to pull income forward, and keep a reserve to cover a wet June. Clubs should agree the season's budget with the committee in the autumn so that winter spending is not held up by meetings.

## Weather contingency

Decide in advance what happens when the weather stops you. For each course type, write down the fallback: a shorebased session, a rebooking offer, a refund policy. Communicate it at the point of booking. Having the policy agreed before the first cancelled day removes the arguments and protects your reviews.

## Keep the plan visible

A plan in the Principal's head is not a plan. Put the year on a wall planner or a shared online calendar that staff and committee can see, with the course programme, the maintenance schedule, the staff training dates and the key deadlines. Review it monthly. When something changes, change the plan, and tell people.

## A one-page template

1. Peak weeks and the capacity they need.
2. Course programme by month, with target numbers.
3. Staffing needs by week and who fills them.
4. Fleet and site work by month, with owners.
5. Compliance dates: recognition renewal, insurance, certificates, policy reviews.
6. Marketing calendar aligned with when people book.
7. Cash forecast by month.
8. Weather and contingency policies.

Centres that plan this way do not have easier seasons; the weather and the customers see to that. What they have is a season where the problems are the unavoidable ones, rather than the ones that were visible in October and left until July.`,
