    excerpt: "Staff too lean and you risk safety; too heavy and you kill your margins. How to find the right number for each session, every time.",
    tags: ["staffing", "ratios", "operations"],
    coverEmoji: "👥",
    imageQuery: "group of sailing instructors team on beach",
    body: `Staffing is the biggest controllable cost in a sailing centre and the biggest single factor in whether a session is safe. Too few instructors and you breach your ratios, overload your safety cover and exhaust your team. Too many and the margin on the course disappears. Most centres land somewhere in the middle by instinct. This article sets out a method for working it out properly, session by session and season by season, so that the numbers on your roster are the right ones for defensible reasons.

## Start from the ratio, not the headcount

The RYA's guidance for each scheme sets out the maximum number of learners per instructor and the safety cover required, varying by activity, level, boat type and the nature of your water. Your operating procedures should state the figures you have adopted, which may be more conservative than the guidance. Those figures, not habit, are the starting point for every staffing decision.

For each session type, write down: learners per instructor, number of instructors required at your maximum group size, powered safety craft required and who drives them, and any additional roles such as a shore contact or a senior instructor in overall charge. The total is the minimum staffing for that session at full capacity. Nothing on the roster should go below it.

## Add the roles that are not teaching

The ratio counts instructors with learners. It does not count the person who needs to be on the shore with a radio and the emergency plan, the person who is covering the office and the phone, the person who can step into a boat if an instructor is hurt, or the Senior Instructor whose attention needs to be on the whole site rather than one group. In a small centre these roles are often filled by the same person, and sometimes that is fine. But when the Principal is also driving a safety boat, there is no one managing the site. Decide which non-teaching roles each day requires and staff them explicitly.

## Account for the boats and the water

Ratios assume the instructor can actually supervise their group. A group of six in single-handers spread across a large estuary is a different supervisory task from six in three double-handers on a small lake. Strong tides, commercial traffic, cold water, long distances to the shore and poor visibility all argue for more cover, and your procedures should say so. Equally, an instructor aboard a stable double-hander with two beginners may be able to take more responsibility than the same instructor shepherding single-handers from a safety boat.

## Plan for the busiest realistic day, then staff the real one

Build a staffing model for your peak: every group running, every boat on the water, with the ratios met and the non-teaching roles covered. That is the number of instructors you need available on your busiest days, plus a margin for sickness, which in a seasonal team is rarely below ten percent. Then, for each actual day, staff to the bookings you have rather than to the peak, with a rule about when a group is confirmed and when an extra instructor is called in.

This is where good booking data pays for itself. If you know on Tuesday that Saturday's beginner course has five booked and two provisional, you can decide with confidence whether you need one instructor or two.

## Watch the hidden costs of under-staffing

Running lean looks efficient on the payroll report and expensive everywhere else. Instructors who never get a break make more mistakes and leave at the end of the season. Sessions that run over ratio because someone did not turn up are the ones that end in incidents and insurance claims. Groups that are too large for the instructor produce learners who do not progress and do not rebook. The Principal who covers every gap personally burns out. Add these to the calculation.

## And the costs of over-staffing

Instructors standing on the slipway with nothing to do cost money and morale. An over-staffed centre also tends to have blurred responsibilities, because nobody is sure who is in charge of what. If you consistently have more people than the sessions need, convert the surplus into value: run a second group, offer private lessons, send the spare instructor to shadow a senior for development, or shorten the roster.

## Match skills, not just numbers

Six instructors are not interchangeable. Each session needs the right mix: a Senior Instructor if the scheme or your procedures require one, a Powerboat Level 2 or Safety Boat qualified driver for each safety craft, an instructor with the right endorsement for the boat type, a first aider within reach, a female instructor for a girls' group if you have promised one. Rostering software that knows each instructor's qualifications and flags the gaps is worth having for this alone; doing it by memory with twenty seasonal staff is where mistakes creep in.

## Use assistants and trainees wisely

Assistant instructors and instructors in training add real value, but they do not count towards the qualified ratio and they need supervision themselves. Use them to improve the experience within an already compliant session, not to stretch it. Be clear in your procedures about what an assistant may and may not do, and brief them and the instructors they work with.

## Review the numbers each season

At the end of the season, look at the actual staffing against the bookings for each session type. Where were you consistently over, where under, and what did it cost in either direction? Where did ratios come under pressure, and why? Were there sessions that only ran because someone did an unsafe number of hours? Feed the answers back into next year's model, your recruitment targets and your pricing, because if a course cannot be run safely at the price you charge, the price is wrong.

## A worked habit, not a formula

There is no single right number of instructors for a centre. There is a right number for each session, found by starting from the ratio, adding the roles the ratio ignores, adjusting for your water and boats, staffing to real bookings with a margin, and matching qualifications to needs. Build that habit into how the roster is made and the staffing question answers itself most weeks, leaving your attention for the weeks when it does not.`,
